summary:
- topic: Administrator Compensation Controversy
  text: The Board Chair opened the meeting by defending a recent, un-agendized vote
    to provide a $12,000 retirement contribution to Assistant Superintendent Dr. Jo
    Prince, citing her extensive extra work on the budget, labor contracts, and school
    reconfiguration. This decision drew significant criticism during public comment
    from teachers and parents, who called it "tone deaf" and a "slap in the face"
    given recent budget cuts, staff reductions, and the closure of Kaler Elementary.
- topic: Public Comment on Budget and Morale
  text: Multiple speakers, including retiring staff, teachers, and union representatives,
    expressed deep disappointment with the board's priorities. They contrasted the
    administrator's compensation with frozen budgets, cuts to student programs like
    music and composting, underpaid Ed Techs, and the emotional toll of staff layoffs,
    arguing it eroded trust and demonstrated a lack of shared sacrifice.
- topic: School Operations and Transportation
  text: The Director of Operations presented a new Heat Mitigation Plan with clear
    temperature thresholds for deploying AC units, developed after recent heat waves
    caused issues. He also addressed transportation concerns, explaining that overcrowding
    on track team and Brick Hill neighborhood buses was being addressed with additional
    contracted buses and a comprehensive route review for the upcoming school year.
- topic: New Student Representatives and School Updates
  text: The board welcomed new student representative Sarah Lupien, who provided a
    detailed report on end-of-year activities across all district schools, including
    field days, award ceremonies, and athletic achievements like the boys' track team
    winning the state championship.
- topic: New Policies Approved
  text: The board unanimously approved a new Educational Equity Policy, developed
    by the district's equity committee to provide a framework for addressing inequities
    using data. They also approved a new Agenda Policy (BEDB) and held the first read
    of a state-mandated cell phone policy (JICI) that will prohibit phone use during
    the school day, with a public workshop planned to develop procedures.
- topic: Education Foundation Update
  text: Parent Sarah Stickney and SPMS Principal Rebecca Stern reported that the new
    South Portland Education Foundation is now officially incorporated. The foundation
    aims to support the district's community school strategy and will focus on building
    its board and fundraising over the summer.
- topic: FY27 Budget Calendar
  text: Following public comment criticism that the proposed 2026-27 meeting calendar
    started budget workshops too late in March, the board voted to amend the schedule
    to officially begin the budget process in December 2026.
